              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Overview\nUnsafe Deserialization occurs when an application deserializes untrusted data\nwithout sufficient validation. Java's native serialization mechanism\n(`ObjectInputStream`) is particularly dangerous because the object stream can\ninclude not just data, but also class information. An attacker can craft a\nmalicious serialized object that, when deserialized, forces the application to\nexecute unintended code, often leading to Remote Code Execution (RCE).\n\nThis type of attack typically uses \"gadget chains,\" which are sequences of\nmethod calls on existing, legitimate classes within the application's libraries\nthat produce a malicious outcome. This vulnerability is categorized as CWE-502:\nDeserialization of Untrusted Data.\n\n### Vulnerable example\nIn this example, a servlet decodes a Base64 string from a user request and\ndeserializes it directly using `ObjectInputStream.readObject()`. Since the\ninput comes from an untrusted source, an attacker can provide a malicious\nobject to take control of the server.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_vuln extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        // SOURCE: Untrusted data is read from the HTTP request.\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            // SINK: Data is deserialized without validation.\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Remediation\nThe safest approach is to avoid native Java serialization and use a safe,\nstructured data format like JSON instead. If you must deserialize native Java\nobjects, use an `ObjectInputFilter` to create an allowlist of classes that are\npermitted for deserialization. This prevents the instantiation of unexpected or\nmalicious \"gadget\" classes.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputFilter;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_safe extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n\n            // REMEDIATION: Apply a filter to allow only expected classes.\n            ObjectInputFilter filter = ObjectInputFilter.Config\n                .createFilter(\"com.example.PondConfig;!*\");\n            in.setObjectInputFilter(filter);\n\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
                "text": "### Overview\nUnsafe Deserialization occurs when an application deserializes untrusted data\nwithout sufficient validation. Java's native serialization mechanism\n(`ObjectInputStream`) is particularly dangerous because the object stream can\ninclude not just data, but also class information. An attacker can craft a\nmalicious serialized object that, when deserialized, forces the application to\nexecute unintended code, often leading to Remote Code Execution (RCE).\n\nThis type of attack typically uses \"gadget chains,\" which are sequences of\nmethod calls on existing, legitimate classes within the application's libraries\nthat produce a malicious outcome. This vulnerability is categorized as CWE-502:\nDeserialization of Untrusted Data.\n\n### Vulnerable example\nIn this example, a servlet decodes a Base64 string from a user request and\ndeserializes it directly using `ObjectInputStream.readObject()`. Since the\ninput comes from an untrusted source, an attacker can provide a malicious\nobject to take control of the server.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_vuln extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        // SOURCE: Untrusted data is read from the HTTP request.\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            // SINK: Data is deserialized without validation.\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Remediation\nThe safest approach is to avoid native Java serialization and use a safe,\nstructured data format like JSON instead. If you must deserialize native Java\nobjects, use an `ObjectInputFilter` to create an allowlist of classes that are\npermitted for deserialization. This prevents the instantiation of unexpected or\nmalicious \"gadget\" classes.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputFilter;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_safe extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n\n            // REMEDIATION: Apply a filter to allow only expected classes.\n            ObjectInputFilter filter = ObjectInputFilter.Config\n                .createFilter(\"com.example.PondConfig;!*\");\n            in.setObjectInputFilter(filter);\n\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "### Overview\nUnsafe Deserialization occurs when an application deserializes untrusted data\nwithout sufficient validation. Java's native serialization mechanism\n(`ObjectInputStream`) is particularly dangerous because the object stream can\ninclude not just data, but also class information. An attacker can craft a\nmalicious serialized object that, when deserialized, forces the application to\nexecute unintended code, often leading to Remote Code Execution (RCE).\n\nThis type of attack typically uses \"gadget chains,\" which are sequences of\nmethod calls on existing, legitimate classes within the application's libraries\nthat produce a malicious outcome. This vulnerability is categorized as CWE-502:\nDeserialization of Untrusted Data.\n\n### Vulnerable example\nIn this example, a servlet decodes a Base64 string from a user request and\ndeserializes it directly using `ObjectInputStream.readObject()`. Since the\ninput comes from an untrusted source, an attacker can provide a malicious\nobject to take control of the server.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_vuln extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        // SOURCE: Untrusted data is read from the HTTP request.\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            // SINK: Data is deserialized without validation.\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Remediation\nThe safest approach is to avoid native Java serialization and use a safe,\nstructured data format like JSON instead. If you must deserialize native Java\nobjects, use an `ObjectInputFilter` to create an allowlist of classes that are\npermitted for deserialization. This prevents the instantiation of unexpected or\nmalicious \"gadget\" classes.\n\n```java\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;\nimport jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputFilter;\nimport java.io.ObjectInputStream;\nimport java.io.Serializable;\nimport java.util.Base64;\n\nclass PondConfig implements Serializable { private String name; }\n\npublic class unsafe_deserialization_safe extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)\n            throws IOException {\n        String configBase64 = request.getParameter(\"config\");\n        byte[] configData = Base64.getDecoder().decode(configBase64);\n\n        ByteArrayInputStream frogConfigStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(configData);\n        try {\n            ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(frogConfigStream);\n\n            // REMEDIATION: Apply a filter to allow only expected classes.\n            ObjectInputFilter filter = ObjectInputFilter.Config\n                .createFilter(\"com.example.PondConfig;!*\");\n            in.setObjectInputFilter(filter);\n\n            PondConfig config = (PondConfig) in.readObject();\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {\n            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"
