                "markdown": "### Overview\nSensitive Cookie with Improper SameSite Attribute in JavaScript refers\nto the incorrect configuration of the SameSite attribute for cookies,\npotentially leading to security vulnerabilities. When not properly configured,\nsensitive information within cookies may be exposed to unauthorized access or\nexploitation. To enhance security, it is crucial to follow best practices by\ncorrectly setting the SameSite attribute.\n\n### Vulnerable Example\n```javascript\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n      sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nIn this example, the SameSite attribute is improperly configured\nas \"None,\" potentially exposing sensitive information.\n\n### Remediation\n```diff\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n-     sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n+     sameSite: \"Lax\", // Corrected\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nBy setting the SameSite attribute to \"Lax,\" the cookie is restricted\nto top-level navigations and subsequent GET requests, enhancing security.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn the vulnerable example, the SameSite attribute is set to \"None,\"\nallowing the cookie to be sent with both same-site and cross-site requests.\nThe remediation involves changing it to \"Lax,\" which ensures the cookie is\nonly sent with top-level navigations, reducing the risk of cross-site\nrequest vulnerabilities.\nIt is essential to configure the SameSite attribute appropriately based on\nthe specific security requirements of your application.\n",
                "text": "### Overview\nSensitive Cookie with Improper SameSite Attribute in JavaScript refers\nto the incorrect configuration of the SameSite attribute for cookies,\npotentially leading to security vulnerabilities. When not properly configured,\nsensitive information within cookies may be exposed to unauthorized access or\nexploitation. To enhance security, it is crucial to follow best practices by\ncorrectly setting the SameSite attribute.\n\n### Vulnerable Example\n```javascript\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n      sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nIn this example, the SameSite attribute is improperly configured\nas \"None,\" potentially exposing sensitive information.\n\n### Remediation\n```diff\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n-     sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n+     sameSite: \"Lax\", // Corrected\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nBy setting the SameSite attribute to \"Lax,\" the cookie is restricted\nto top-level navigations and subsequent GET requests, enhancing security.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn the vulnerable example, the SameSite attribute is set to \"None,\"\nallowing the cookie to be sent with both same-site and cross-site requests.\nThe remediation involves changing it to \"Lax,\" which ensures the cookie is\nonly sent with top-level navigations, reducing the risk of cross-site\nrequest vulnerabilities.\nIt is essential to configure the SameSite attribute appropriately based on\nthe specific security requirements of your application.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "### Overview\nSensitive Cookie with Improper SameSite Attribute in JavaScript refers\nto the incorrect configuration of the SameSite attribute for cookies,\npotentially leading to security vulnerabilities. When not properly configured,\nsensitive information within cookies may be exposed to unauthorized access or\nexploitation. To enhance security, it is crucial to follow best practices by\ncorrectly setting the SameSite attribute.\n\n### Vulnerable Example\n```javascript\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n      sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nIn this example, the SameSite attribute is improperly configured\nas \"None,\" potentially exposing sensitive information.\n\n### Remediation\n```diff\nvar express = require(\"express\");\nvar session = require(\"express-session\");\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    cookie: {\n      domain: \"domain.org\",\n      secure: true,\n      httpOnly: true,\n-     sameSite: \"None\", // Vulnerable\n+     sameSite: \"Lax\", // Corrected\n      name: \"sensitive_cookie\",\n      maxAge: 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n      path: \"/\",\n    },\n  })\n);\n```\nBy setting the SameSite attribute to \"Lax,\" the cookie is restricted\nto top-level navigations and subsequent GET requests, enhancing security.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn the vulnerable example, the SameSite attribute is set to \"None,\"\nallowing the cookie to be sent with both same-site and cross-site requests.\nThe remediation involves changing it to \"Lax,\" which ensures the cookie is\nonly sent with top-level navigations, reducing the risk of cross-site\nrequest vulnerabilities.\nIt is essential to configure the SameSite attribute appropriately based on\nthe specific security requirements of your application.\n"
